<h2 id="proposer">What already exists, and what this page is about</h2>

<p>The Reality Code Engine already holds the half of the problem that decides. Its public paper, <a href="https://research.openie.dev/papers/rce/">The Reality Code Engine</a>, reports the following, all of it OpenIE's own measured or proved data, not outside evidence. A commit gate checks every candidate at entry and refused all 10,000 malformed payloads and all 10,000 specifications with a flow to an undeclared node in a preregistered run of 10,000 generated Forms with no model called. Replay is exact: theorem T3 states that the runtime state is a left fold of the transition function over the log, and that replay reproduces every hash and output; T1 (determinism), T2 (totality with a step bound) and T8 (a refused event leaves the state unchanged) are proved in Lean 4, a proof assistant. Projection to other languages is built: T9-F says the Rust, WebAssembly (WASM), Python, XState and SCXML (State Chart XML) targets print the reference trace byte for byte, established by differential testing on 529 events of one eleven-operator specification, and in the 10,000-Form run two compiles gave the same bytes and the XState and SCXML projections of the original and round-tripped Form were byte identical in 10,000 of 10,000 cases, while the emitted Python printed the reference trace in 9,999 of 10,000. T9-L, the Rust program emitter, is proved on a core as a verified translation into MiniRust. Kani, a model checker for Rust, verifies 10 of 10 kernel harnesses, and every built-in rewrite of the e-graph optimizer is proved in Z3, a satisfiability modulo theories (SMT) solver. Every run carries <code>est_j</code>, an estimate in joules from a published cost table, kept apart from counter readings (<code>reported_j</code>) and wall-meter readings (<code>measured_j</code>).</p>

<p><strong>This page is about the proposer:</strong> the learned part that suggests which Form to try. The gate, replay, projections and <code>est_j</code> are built and measured; the proposer is not.</p>

<h3 id="steer">The model steers; the engine renders</h3>

<p>Robotics already works this way. In V-JEPA 2-AC, the action-conditioned version of the V-JEPA 2 video model, a planner searches over robot actions by minimizing the distance between a predicted future representation and the representation of a goal image; it never generates pixels [B1]. The encoder was pretrained on more than one million hours of video, and the action-conditioned predictor on fewer than 62 hours of unlabeled robot video [B1, abstract]. RCE-JEPA proposes the same division for code: a learned model chooses edits to a Form, and the engine renders every target language from the accepted Form. No model emits code.</p>

<p>Code differs from robotics in one decisive way. A robot's world model must <em>learn</em> how the world moves, <span set:html={m(R`z_{t+1}=g_\phi(z_t,a_t)`)} />. In RCE the transition function <span set:html={m(R`\delta`)} /> is given and proved, so the forward dynamics need not be learned at all. What remains to learn is the inverse: which edit moves a candidate toward what the intent requires. MuZero learns a model because, as its authors put it, planning methods "rely on knowledge of the environment's dynamics" [T13, Sec. 1]; here those dynamics are written down.</p>

<h3 id="one-form">One real Form</h3>

<p>Before the architecture, the object. This is a complete program from the engine repository (<code>crates/rce/examples/surface/counter.rce</code>), fourteen lines in RCE's surface syntax, shown unedited:</p>

<pre class="mt-4 p-4 rounded bg-black/40 text-xs leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{`intent "a counter that never goes negative"
    example inc, inc, dec -> 1
    example dec -> refused: Underflow

state Counter
    n: u64 = 0

event Inc
event Dec

error Underflow

step Counter on Inc -> n + 1
step Counter on Dec -> try n.checked_sub(1) else Underflow`}</code></pre>

<p>Read it as <span set:html={m(R`P=(S,E,\delta,D,\lambda)`)} />. The <code>state</code> block is S, one unsigned 64-bit integer starting at zero. The two <code>event</code> lines are E. The two <code>step</code> lines are <span set:html={m(R`\delta`)} />. The <code>intent</code> block is a small specification of intent, written <span set:html={m(R`\sigma(\iota)`)} /> on this page: two required traces, one of which must be refused. A decrement at zero is refused with <code>Underflow</code> and, by T8, leaves the state unchanged. The proposer's task, stated on this example: given only the <code>intent</code> block, find the <code>state</code>, <code>event</code> and <code>step</code> lines that the gate accepts.</p>

