<p><strong>Not learned, ever:</strong> <span set:html={m(R`\delta`)} />, <span set:html={m(R`\lambda`)} />, the fold, the hash chain, the projections, the gate and the promotion path. They are fixed and certified, and promotion never retrains them.</p>

<h3 id="decision-head">The decision head is a proposer, and it sits outside the gate</h3>

<p>A class of small, energy-aware decision models appeared in 2026. A decision model takes a piece of state and a fixed question and returns a typed answer with a probability for every option, without generating text; all questions about one state are answered in a single request [B26; B30]. Answers are bounded: a yes or no, a choice among at most 255 options, or a score on a declared scale [B29]. Members of the class, cited here as convergent examples of the concept and not as components of RCE:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Laya</strong>, an open-weight model of about 421 million parameters built on a ModernBERT-large encoder with a two-layer decision head, released under Apache 2.0 [B29, B31].</li>
  <li><strong>Kev</strong>, a family of open decision models from 0.8 to 27 billion parameters built on Qwen backbones, each checkpoint shipped with a fitted temperature for calibration [B32].</li>
  <li><strong>Liquid AI's open d1 models</strong>, d1-3B and d1-omni-600M, released on 7 October 2026 for edge and physical systems: d1-3B answers one question in 16 ms on an NVIDIA Jetson AGX Thor and 50 ms on a Jetson Orin Nano, reads camera frames, and was shown navigating an environment in the Isaac Sim robot simulator with the model served on a Jetson in a hardware-in-the-loop setup [B33].</li>
</ul>
<p>An independent benchmark of one hosted member of the class found its choice probabilities well calibrated, with an expected calibration error (ECE) of 0.028 pooled over 22 choice datasets and 279,925 answers [B26]; its binary (yes or no) probabilities were less well placed (ECE 0.052 single, 0.168 multi-label) and needed tuned thresholds [B26, Sec. 4.3]. Documented weaknesses of the class include counting, arithmetic and date comparison, and lower accuracy when the state is padded with irrelevant content [B29, B30].</p>
<p><strong>What the RCE project plans.</strong> The project will build its own decision head, trained and calibrated on its own gate receipts. Every gate decision already writes a receipt with the candidate, the outcome, the broken rule and <code>est_j</code>, so calibration data costs no labelling. The state handed to the head is the Form's canonical JSON and its trace, which is small by construction; that addresses the documented padded-state weakness directly. The head is a proposer. Any decision model, hosted or local, built by the project or by anyone else, only proposes; it never admits a Form, and the security of RCE never depends on it.</p>

<h3 id="data">Data</h3>
<p>V-JEPA 2 pairs very large passive pretraining with a small action-conditioned set [B1]. The RCE analogue has four tiers.</p>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Source</th><th>Size target</th><th>Trains</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Passive: Forms and traces</td><td>Forms lifted from pinned public corpora by the engine's lift pipeline (<code>rce scan DIR --form</code>), the seeded 10,000-Form generator, and every promoted handler</td><td><span set:html={m(R`10^5`)} /> to <span set:html={m(R`10^6`)} /> Forms with traces</td><td>The Form encoder, by predicting masked rules and masked trace segments in latent space</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Paired: intent and fit</td><td>Tests and contracts attached to lifted programs; a Form's own trace as a self-generated specification; the preregistered brownfield applications; benchmark specifications written as Forms</td><td><span set:html={m(R`10^4`)} /> pairs to start</td><td>Intent encoder and proposer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decision receipts</td><td>Every gate decision</td><td>Unbounded, no labelling cost</td><td>The decision head's calibration</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hard negatives</td><td>Gate rejections with the named broken rule; model-proposed equivalences the gate refused (13 of 16 from Qwen2.5-Coder-1.5B in the RCE paper, Sec. 5.5)</td><td>Grows with every run</td><td>Contrastive signal: valid against invalid, equivalent against not</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
<p>Near-duplicate Forms are removed by shape hash before any split, and held-out splits are by shape hash, never by surface text. The 83-family corpus quoted under T2 below is kept as the historical baseline only; it sets no thresholds and trains no model. The lift corpus replaces it as the main evidence as soon as the data engine (Phase 0 of the pilot plan) produces it.</p>

<h3 id="training">Training, in brief</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Encoder objective.</strong> Latent prediction plus SIGReg, the recipe of LeJEPA, which needs a single trade-off hyperparameter and no stop-gradient or teacher-student scheme [B2, abstract], and of LeWorldModel, which trains end to end with two loss terms [B3, abstract]. Two cautions from the same line of work: the LeWorldModel authors flag the isotropic Gaussian target as a possible limitation in environments of low intrinsic dimension [B3, Sec. 4.2 and Sec. 6], and contrastive inverse dynamics is offered as a distribution-free alternative [B4], which, however, was evaluated on continuous controls only and was clearly worse than SIGReg on combinatorial puzzle tasks whose discrete state changes look alike whichever action caused them [B4, Appendix D]; and SIGReg and VISReg "do not guarantee that the learned latent representation preserves all the key information required for planning" [B5, Sec. 4.1]. Both arms run side by side, with auxiliary heads that predict exact, cheap properties of the Form (monotone tag, fuel bound, rule count).</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-step prediction.</strong> Training with k-step recursive prediction gives significantly better results in the EB-JEPA ablations, by reducing exposure bias, with a Pareto optimum near k = 4 [B14, Fig. 3]; RoboJEPA raises the autoregressive training rollout from K = 2 to K = 10 steps in a long-horizon cooldown, which pays off only at large compute (above about 10<sup>21</sup> FLOPs) [B8, Sec. 3.3 and App. C.2]; a rollout-consistency term matched to the planner horizon comes from Temporal-Distance JEPA [B6, abstract].</li>
  <li><strong>Monotone energy.</strong> A monotone cost ranking makes plans that deviate further from a goal-reaching trajectory cost more; without it, local minima in the cost surface can lead the planner to suboptimal plans [B7]. Monotonicity is not guaranteed by the task: the EB-JEPA navigation task is non-monotonic, since the best path first moves away from the goal [B14]. Here it is a preregistered diagnostic (H11), not an assumption.</li>
  <li><strong>Proposer.</strong> Supervised on intent, Form and edit triples from the paired tier, then improved on its own gate-accepted trajectories: the gate is the reward.</li>
  <li><strong>Never decode a latent directly.</strong> MoRAE reports that generating motion directly in a frozen JEPA latent "fails dramatically", and traces the failure to an ill-conditioned latent spectrum and to small latent errors amplified into large artifacts after decoding [B16, abstract and Sec. 3]. The proposer outputs typed edits; latents rank, they do not render.</li>
</ul>

