<h3 id="falsifiers">Falsifiers for the premise itself</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Observation</th><th>What it defeats</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>RJ-F1</td><td>Lift coverage plateaus below 80% on any of the five target languages after the construct list is exhausted</td><td>The claim that all code can be emitted from RCE, and with it the scope of autonomous coding over Forms</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RJ-F2</td><td>Writing <span set:html={m(R`\sigma(\iota)`)} /> for the 7 reference applications costs more person-hours than writing their Forms by hand</td><td>The claim that closure removes, rather than moves, the specification cost</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RJ-F3</td><td>H3 fails while the token arm passes at the same gate</td><td>Latent proposal over Form space; the gate still stands, the JEPA half does not</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RJ-F4</td><td>H11 fails and search does not reach accepted Forms within the beam budget</td><td>Energy-guided planning in Form space</td></tr>
    <tr><td>RJ-F5</td><td>The decision head's calibration never beats a frequency table over shape hashes</td><td>The decision-model leg; it adds nothing over promoted-handler statistics</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="risks">Risks and mitigations</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Mitigation</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Decoding a latent amplifies small errors</td><td>Direct generation in a frozen JEPA latent fails dramatically for motion, from spectral ill-conditioning and decoder amplification [B16]</td><td>Never decode a latent; the proposer emits typed edits and the gate checks them</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A representation that does not collapse but learns the wrong thing</td><td>"Physical representation laziness" under SIGReg [B5]</td><td>Auxiliary exact-property heads; an inverse-dynamics regularizer</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Isotropic prior misfits a low-dimensional Form space</td><td>[B3]; [B4]</td><td>SIGReg and contrastive inverse-dynamics arms side by side, remembering that the inverse-dynamics arm was weaker on discrete puzzle state [B4]</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Energy terms not commensurate</td><td>[T18, Sec. 1.3]</td><td>Fit <span set:html={m(R`\alpha,\beta,\gamma`)} /> per experiment and publish them</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decision head degrades on padded state</td><td>Documented weakness of the class [B29, B30]</td><td>Feed only canonical Form JSON and trace</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Expressiveness ceiling</td><td>Rice's theorem [T20]; the restricted language</td><td>Lift coverage is the measurement; RJ-F1 is the falsifier</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Specification cost dominates</td><td>seL4 effort (Section 2 below); invariant under-constraint [B23]</td><td>RJ-F2; tools that capture traces from running programs and infer contracts, as deliverables in their own right</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<p>The phased pilot plan, from the data engine through convergence, with preregistration and exit criteria for each phase, is kept with the project's working notes and summarized here: Phase 0 builds the lift corpus and receipt dataset; Phase 1 trains the Form encoder and tests H1, H5 and H11; Phase 2 builds and calibrates the decision head (H12); Phase 3 trains the proposer and planner against the token control (H3, H4, H10, H13); Phase 4 measures energy and determinism on a wall meter (H2, H8, H9); Phase 5 runs the convergence protocol, in which promotion takes the model out of the loop for each closed shape.</p>

